cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you analyse or use BRI-410, please cite it as below."
title: "BRI-410: A 512-bit Tweakable Block Cipher with Key- and Tweak-Dependent Permutations"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Rinald
    family-names: Cobaj
    affiliation: Independent researcher
    email: vlorarino@gmail.com
version: "1.1"
date-released: "2026-10-27"
license: CC0-1.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/rinaldcobaj-cell/BRI-410"
abstract: >-
  BRI-410 is an experimental (study) tweakable block cipher with a 512-bit block,
  a 410-bit key, a 128-bit tweak and 16 rounds, with BRI-410 AE, an instantiation
  of the ThetaCB3 authenticated-encryption mode with a 416-bit tag. In every round,
  the key and the tweak select the permutation layer from a family of 32
  "knight-move" permutations that are diffusion-optimal in both directions. The
  wide-trail bound of 81 active S-boxes over four rounds is proven for every
  sequence of these permutations; it concerns individual differential
  characteristics and linear trails, not the overall security of the cipher.
  Version 1.0 is kept in the record: in it, three consecutive round keys of one
  block revealed the master key. Version 1.1 fixes this with a Davies-Meyer
  feed-forward. This archive contains the specification, the papers (version 1
  and version 2), a reference implementation, test vectors and every analysis
  script. BRI-410 has not been analysed by independent cryptanalysts and must not
  be used to protect real data.
keywords:
  - tweakable block cipher
  - substitution-permutation network
  - wide-trail strategy
  - key-dependent permutation
  - authenticated encryption
  - cryptanalysis
